The facts check out but the conclusion does not. Verified: `grep -rn "specificityRule|isCompletenessRule|namedEscalationTrigger|watchScheduleShortfall"` across the whole repo returns exactly the definition (server/src/audit/completeness.ts:219), the schema declaration (schema/binder-schema-v1.json:887-896), and four lines in server/test/completeness.test.ts. No production caller; `assessSlot` (completeness.ts:249+) never touches `slot.specificityRule`. All confirmed.

What kills it as a defect is that the rule's SUBJECT deliberately does not exist, and the repo says so three times in code plus once in doctrine:

1. server/src/audit/run.ts:470-474 — the record-set evidence builder, the only thing that could feed s10.concerns:
       if (slot.id !== 's7.components') {
         // Every other record-set is keyed on something this builder does not hold
         // yet — lab results, concerns (Increment 5, gated on v4), programs.
         return { evidence: { expected: 0, withRecord: 0, shortfalls: [] } }
       }
   The expectation set for concerns is empty by construction. A per-record rule has zero records to run against.

2. server/src/plan/sessionPlan.ts:769-772 states the exact pattern this finding objects to, as a deliberate practice:
       openConcerns: { count: 0,
         note: 'recorded, not specced — concerns are Increment 5 and gated on manifest v4. The key ' +
           'exists so the shape has room; nothing writes to it.' }
   Plus sessionPlan.ts:314 `openConcerns: never[]` and migrations/003_overlays.sql:58 ("v4 adds concerns"), 008_operators.sql:7 ("5 the concern register").

3. CLAUDE.md §15 lists concerns-as-record under "What this file describes that nothing yet implements" and rules: "None of these is a defect. Every one is real, wanted, and correctly not built yet."

4. The schema-side half is not an outlier either. Counting readers of the declarative keys on that very slot: `recordFields` 0, `aiMayDraft` 0, `neverAI` 0, `completeWhen` 0 — zero production and zero test hits each. `/schema` is "the Master Spec as machine-readable data" (CLAUDE.md §13), and server/src/audit/schema.ts:112 already labels a schema field "Documentation only." An unread `specificityRule` is the normal condition of the declarative half of a slot, not a dropped wire.

5. The Increment 3 build spec asked for precisely what shipped — docs/…Increment-3_Build-Spec_2026-07-27.md:207-210, "Two rules that are easy to miss and **are tested**", naming the locating-photo rule and §10's specificity rule. The locating-photo sibling is wired (completeness.ts:201-205, via field-item bindings, §1c "bind, do not re-implement"); this one cannot be, because its entity is v4.

This is also not the signature failure class. That class is a value that IS computed on a live path with no reader. Here nothing is computed on any path, because no concern record can exist to compute it from.

The claim states "read by nothing — not by any renderer, not by a test" and names its own disproof: "It would be disproved by any SELECT naming the column... none of which exist." That SELECT exists. server/test/resolve-product.test.ts:193-198 runs the real write path (runResolveProduct) and then reads the column back:

    const row = db.prepare('SELECT product, kind, honesty, resolved FROM product_resolutions').get() as {
      product: string; kind: string; honesty: string; resolved: number
    }
    assert.deepEqual(row, {
      product: 'Burcam Series 600 captive-air pressure tank', kind: 'equipment', honesty: 'Inferred', resolved: 1,
    })

The suite runs it: `npx tsx --test server/test/resolve-product.test.ts` reports 20 tests, 20 pass, 0 skipped.

The claim's load-bearing drift argument — that the SQL literal and HONESTY_LABELS are "a hand-kept restatement of each other... with nothing coupling it" — is falsified by mutation. I edited server/src/ai/tasks/resolveProduct.ts:327 from 'Inferred' to 'Documented' and reran: "not ok 1 - sends NO IMAGES..." / "# pass 19 / # fail 1". The exact drift the finding predicts would go unnoticed (the pass emitting `Documented`, which lookup.ts:190 calls "structurally unreachable") fails a test on contact. I reverted with git checkout; resolveProduct.ts:327 is back to 'Inferred' and is not in git status.

The "no renderer" half is true but not distinctive. Nothing renders this table at all — server/scripts/resolve.ts:158 is the only human-facing display and CLAUDE.md §15 records "Rendering the binder itself — Nothing", with the standing rule that correctly-not-built-yet is not a defect. product/kind/specificity/recognised_from are equally unrendered.

Nor is the honesty label as a whole stranded in this repo. The label that actually reaches a signed human decision travels a live path: server/src/engine/confirm.ts:43-46 (`ACT_LABEL = { confirmed: 'Observed', adopted: 'Inferred' }`), written at confirm.ts:137-143 into object_provenance.honesty_label and constrained by server/src/db/migrations/018_object_confirmation.sql:106-107 (`(act = 'confirmed' AND honesty_label = 'Observed') OR (act = 'adopted' AND honesty_label = 'Inferred')`). Doctrine 2 is not unenforced here.

This does not match the signature failure class. That class is a value computed with no reader and no test on the path — here the stored value has an executing test that fails when the path changes.
